course: dictionary
lesson: 5893
title: One word, several meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 3
explain: The same word can have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one. Mao with a capital letter is usually a name. «Mei's grandfather says Mao made China strong; her grandmother, who was sent to a village as a girl, remembers it differently.» Here Mao is a person, and two people remember him in different ways.
explain: Some events in a person's life are described in very different ways, so read carefully and ask who is speaking. «China's official view, set out under Deng Xiaoping, is that Mao was "70% right, 30% wrong".» Historians and people today judge Mao in different ways, so a sentence may give one view, not the whole truth.
explain: In Mandarin, the same sounds with different tones mean different things. «Māo» (猫) means cat, and it sounds like "mow". «Máo» (毛) means hair, fur or feather, and it is also a small unit of money. Do not guess a Mandarin word from English: it is not an English word.
explain: To check which meaning is right, ask three questions: Is it a name with a capital letter? Is it a sound from another language? Does the sentence make sense with your meaning put in? If you are not sure, use a dictionary.
fact: Mao is a proper noun, so it is the name of one particular person and starts with a capital letter.
fact: «Mao» usually means Mao Zedong, the leader of the Communist Party, who ruled the People's Republic of China from its start in 1949 until his death.
fact: «Māo» (猫) means cat in Mandarin, and «máo» (毛) means hair, fur or feather. Ten mao make one yuan.
fact: His rule is remembered both for uniting the country and for campaigns in which millions of people died of famine and persecution.
fact: Historians and Chinese people judge Mao in different ways, so read more than one source before you decide what you think.
exercise: «Mei's grandfather says Mao made China strong.» In this sentence, what is Mao? | A cat | A unit of Chinese money | *A name of a person | A kind of hair
exercise: In Mandarin, «māo» means what? | Hair or fur | A kind of soup | Ten pence | *A cat
exercise: In «yí kuài wǔ máo» (one yuan fifty), what is a mao? | A type of feather | A cat | The leader of a country | *A small unit of Chinese money
test: Mei's grandmother remembers Mao differently from her grandfather. What does this show? | Mao was only a cat | Mao has changed his name | *People judge Mao in different ways | Mao was a kind of food
test: Two words sound alike: «māo» and «máo». Which one means a cat? | Neither, they both mean money | *māo | máo | Both mean hair
exam: Read «Mao's Great Leap Forward caused a terrible famine.» What does this sentence teach us about history? | Leaders never make mistakes | *Some events in a leader's rule are remembered as terrible, so read carefully and check more than one source | Famine is a kind of food | Every event in history was happy
